# The themes

The bundled starter set — one config word each (`<config theme=…>` or `--theme …`):

- **`default`** — the academic / print house look: a serif body, a sans heading face, bold headings, a white page. Injects nothing (it *is* the base stylesheet).
- **`modern`** — a clean, web-native retune: sans-serif body, lighter headings, a touch more air, a blue accent. A pure token retune.
- **`compact`** — tighter spacing and smaller type for dense reading and review. A pure token retune.
- **`tufte`** — the sidenote-forward, generous-margin, old-style-serif look of Edward Tufte's books: a Palatino-family serif for body *and* headings, a warm cream page, italic section headings, a classic reading measure, and margin notes as the presented default (pair it with `<config note-position=margin>`). The worked example of a rung-3 full theme — almost entirely a token retune, plus one structural rule (see The Tufte worked example).

The theme name is an enumerable family: the value spec for `<config theme=…>` (the validated allowlist), the engine's `KNOWN_THEMES`, the CLI `--theme` help, and this list are kept in step, and a test asserts the value spec equals the shipped themes directory.

# Dark mode

A theme ships **one (light) token set**; the engine **derives the dark variant automatically** from those tokens (#398 decision, 2026-07-14) — a theme never hand-authors a second palette, and every theme gets a coherent light/dark pair for free. The derivation is **deterministic and baked**: it runs at generation time (`npm run dark:gen`, guarded by `npm run check:dark`) and writes the derived values into the stylesheets themselves — no runtime colour math, no JavaScript required for dark to work.

## The derivation rules

Each token's **Role** (the column in the token reference above) selects its rule. The rules operate in OKLCH — perceptual lightness, **hue always preserved** — so a warm cream page derives to a warm near-black, never a neutral one:

- **surface** — backgrounds darken. The page maps to dark-surface lightness (L 0.215); other surfaces keep their *elevation*, inverted: what sat below the page in light sits above it in dark. Chroma is capped to a whisper — warmth survives, tint does not glare.
- **text** — text lightens and reflects (L′ = 1 − 0.75·L, clamped to the readable band), preserving the emphasis *order*: primary stays brightest, muted stays dimmest.
- **accent** — links, the accent, the error flag, and callout accents keep their hue and rise into the contrast-on-dark band (L′ = 1 − 0.6·L, clamped to [0.65, 0.84]), chroma slightly tamed — vivid, never neon.
- **border** — rules and dividers mute: near-neutral grays whose visibility *order* survives (the further from white in light, the further from black in dark), clamped to a muted band — visible, never crisp.
- **tint** — callout washes become a just-above-surface wash of the same hue.
- **alias** — tokens defined as `var(<another token>)` are never derived; they follow their referent.
- **fixed** — non-colour tokens (fonts, weights, sizes, leading, spacing, layout) pass through untouched: dark never changes typography or geometry.

Every derived palette must pass the **contrast gate** before it can be baked: WCAG-AA floors for the text tiers, links, and the error flag against every surface; the non-text floor for callout accents on their tints; and a visible-border floor (the invisible-border classic, encoded). A palette that fails cannot ship — the generator exits with the failing pairs.

## Correcting a single dial (the per-token override)

Auto-dark is the zero-effort default; when one derived value isn't what your theme wants, override **that token only** — never fork a palette. In your theme file, add one block:

```
:root[data-theme-variant="dark"] {
  --enscribe-link: #8ab4f8;
}
```

The generator reads it, **skips deriving those tokens** (derivation fills only what you didn't), folds your values into both dark scopes (so they hold under OS-auto too), and still runs the contrast gate over the result. One write point; the system makes it total. (For the record: all four shipped themes currently pass the gate on pure derivation — zero overrides.)

## Where dark lives at runtime

The baked block ships under two scopes:

- `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` with `:root:not([data-theme-variant="light"])` — **auto**: the document follows the OS, with zero JavaScript.
- `:root[data-theme-variant="dark"]` — the **explicit hook**: set `data-theme-variant="dark"` (or `"light"`, which defeats the media block) on the document root. This is the reader-tier switch's attachment point — the settings panel (below) drives exactly this attribute; `color-scheme` rides along so native scrollbars and form controls follow.

The **document-tier default** is `<config theme-variant=light|dark|auto>` (default `auto`): `light`/`dark` pin the variant by stamping the attribute on `<html>` in every static build — the single-file render, a static website (site-wide, from the master's `<config>`), and a separate-pages book — and at mount on the live path; `auto` (or omitting it) leaves the OS in charge. An unknown value warns and falls back to `auto` — the standard config behaviour. Per the two-tier model, the document sets this default and the reader's own switch overrides it locally.

## Testing your theme in dark

Three ways, no tooling required: flip your OS appearance (the auto path); add `data-theme-variant="dark"` to `<html>` in the rendered file (the explicit path); or set `<config theme-variant=dark />` while iterating. To check the derived values themselves, `npm run dark:gen -- --report` prints every theme's contrast table. One known boundary: hover-preview tooltips keep their light styling in dark for now (the tooltip layer sits on a vendored stylesheet) — a known boundary of the settings panel (below).

# The settings panel

A **gear** in the shell chrome (beside Edit and the repository mark) opens a small panel with **two tiers** — the concrete expression of the theme system's split between what a document *is* and how a *reader* takes it in.

**The document tier** edits the document's `<config>` block: it is a friendly editor over document state, not a separate store. A theme picker and a default-variant control rewrite `<config theme=…>` / `<config theme-variant=…>` right in the source (visible in the source pane) — the change is the document's, saved with it. This tier appears wherever a single-document source with a `<config>` is being edited: a live article or single-file in edit mode, **and an article page in the website or playground editor** — the playground is the natural place to try a theme on. Static pages and read-only live documents show the reader tier alone; so does a **book** in edit mode, whose live loop edits its chapter files, not the master `<config>` where the theme lives (editing a book's theme is a master-`<config>` affordance, not this per-chapter surface). A website mounted read-only (the docs site as you read it) is reader-tier-only too — the document tier arrives only with the editor.

**The reader tier** is the visitor's own — local to their browser, never touching the document: **text size**, **line spacing**, **text width**, and a **light/dark switch**. It persists per-origin in `localStorage`, applies on load, and offers *reset to theme*. Each control is a CSS custom-property override on the document root (`--enscribe-text-base`, `--enscribe-line-height`, `--enscribe-content-width`) layered *above* the theme's tokens — an inline style outranks every stylesheet `:root` by cascade origin, so theme and reader settings **compose** with no `!important`. The light/dark switch drives the same `data-theme-variant` hook above (auto removes it → the OS decides; light/dark pin it). Because every override lives on the persistent document root, the settings survive a live site's page-to-page navigation.

**The editor layout toggle** (in the chrome corner while editing) flips the editor between **stacked** — the Write/Preview tabs — and **side-by-side** — editor and preview in two columns. Like the reader tier it is a **UI setting, not a `<config>` setting**: how you arrange the editor is your view of the work, not part of the work, so it persists per-origin in `localStorage` and is **never written to the document**. It is offered on the article editors and the playground above a comfortable width, and collapses back to the tabs on a narrow screen.

**Why two tiers — and where the line falls.** The document/theme tier owns *identity*: the theme, the typeface, the default appearance — what the author decides and the document carries. The reader tier owns *legibility of that same design*: how large, how loose, how wide, and the flip between the light and dark variants the theme already provides. The rule that draws the line — **anything that changes the design's identity is document-tier; anything that changes the legibility of the same design is reader-tier** — is why **font family stays document-tier** (it *is* the design) while **text size is reader-tier**. That is the deliberate departure from e-reader convention, which puts typeface reader-side only because those systems have no theme tier to hold it. Precedence follows from the same idea: the document sets the defaults, the reader overrides them locally the way a reader controls their own reading, and *reset to theme* hands control back.

# The Tufte worked example

`tufte` is the reference full theme — proof that even the showpiece is **almost entirely a token retune**. Its `:root` block retunes the font, colour, measure, leading, and heading-scale tokens; that carries the entire look, and the margin notes inherit it through the shared margin layout — no theme rule touches the sidenotes. The theme adds **exactly one** structural rule, and it earns its place because no token can express it:

- **Italic section headings** — `section-title, sub-section-title, sub-sub-section-title { font-style: italic }`, the Tufte section-head idiom. There is no `--enscribe-*` token for `font-style`, and the article and book titles stay roman (so the rule is scoped to the section family). The post-#397 native heading inherits the style through `font: inherit`, so styling the outer element suffices.

Everything else Tufte-ish is a token: the serif is `--enscribe-font-body` / `--enscribe-font-heading`, the light headings are `--enscribe-weight-heading: 400`, the cream page is `--enscribe-bg`, the measure is `--enscribe-content-width`. (One honest edge: the margin-note *marker* stays in the sans face, because the margin layout pins it to `--enscribe-font-sans` and the theme does not override that; a serif marker would need an engine-side token or a structural rule that beats the margin layout — deferred, not needed for the look.)
